{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1770,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fusion of hydrogen or helium normally requires at least the conditions found in the the cores"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7310,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of stars . High temperatures and densities\nallow hydrogen and helium nuclei to get close"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12400,
    "dDurationMs": 4570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough to fuse together into bigger nuclei\nand release a TON of energy, powering even"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16970,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more fusion while releasing enough extra to\npower the star, or if you set this situation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21880,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up on earth, you might have a hydrogen bomb."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24220,
    "dDurationMs": 3970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s actually possible for fusion to\noccur at temperatures much, much lower than"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28190,
    "dDurationMs": 3009,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the core of the sun - like, room temperature,\nfor example."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31199,
    "dDurationMs": 4191,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, I’m not talking about the infamous\n“cold fusion” of the 1980s that hasn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35390,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been shown to work or involve any, well, fusion\n- no, I’m talking about the room-temperature"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40390,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fusion of the 1950s that actually does work:\nfusion with the help of muons!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46990,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nuclear fusion, of course, happens when atomic\nnuclei, like hydrogen nuclei , come close"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51650,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough together that their strong nuclear\nattraction can overcome their electric"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55720,
    "dDurationMs": 4409,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "repulsion, and they fuse together into a single,\nbigger nucleus - like helium ."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60129,
    "dDurationMs": 5121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This typically happens in a plasma, that is,\na super hot soup of electrons and atomic nuclei,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65250,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where if it’s hot enough every once in a\nwhile two nuclei bump hard enough into each"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69110,
    "dDurationMs": 5991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other to fuse . But fusion can in principle\nhappen in regular, non-plasma molecules, too"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75101,
    "dDurationMs": 4919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- like the hydrogen molecule, in which two\nhydrogen nuclei are kept relatively near to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80020,
    "dDurationMs": 4619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "each other by sharing electrons . The nuclei\ndon’t stay separated a rigid distance apart,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84639,
    "dDurationMs": 4281,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "though - they vibrate and wiggle and every\nso often, they can, in principle, get close"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88920,
    "dDurationMs": 1379,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough to fuse together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90299,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But with hydrogen - or nitrogen, or oxygen,\nor pretty much all other molecules - this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94469,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happens exceedingly rarely (which is why our\natmosphere, which has a fair amount of molecules,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99619,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "isn’t a giant fusion bomb)."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101619,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, things are different if you replace\nthe electrons with particles called muons,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107109,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which are basically exactly the same as electrons\nexcept 200 times heavier .  Muons, being"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112549,
    "dDurationMs": 4941,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "essentially heavy electrons, form atoms and\nmolecules in almost the exact same way as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117490,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electrons, but since they’re heavier, their\norbits are much closer to the nucleus than"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121610,
    "dDurationMs": 4169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an electron with the same energy and angular\nmomentum would be . And this means that atoms"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125779,
    "dDurationMs": 5191,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and molecules held together with muons instead\nof electrons are about 200 times smaller,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130970,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and their nuclei are correspondingly about\n200 times closer together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135190,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And being closer together makes nuclei many\nmany many times more likely to fuse together,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141330,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so much so that hydrogen molecules made with\nmuons can fuse together at temperatures much"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146730,
    "dDurationMs": 3340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lower than the core of the sun - even room\ntemperature!!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150070,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which was predicted in 1947 and experimentally\nachieved in 1956 . Physicists have even managed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155750,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to achieve muon-aided nuclear fusion at temperatures\nclose to absolute zero."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160670,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So at this point, you’re probably asking\nyourself: if room-temperature nuclear fusion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164860,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exists, why aren’t we using it to power\nmodern civilization?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168160,
    "dDurationMs": 6450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, while muon-facilitated fusion is indeed\nfully legit nuclear fusion at non-crazy temperatures,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174610,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are some major problems which prevent\nit from being used as a power source."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179080,
    "dDurationMs": 6620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First, muons don’t live very long . Unlike electrons which have an in principle infinite lifespan,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185700,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after\nabout 2 microseconds muons spontaneously decay"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188540,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into an electron and some neutrinos, so if\nyou’re going to do anything with muons,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192870,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you have to do it real quick!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194550,
    "dDurationMs": 3930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This turns out not to matter much for the\npurposes of facilitating fusion, but because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198480,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of their short lifespan, there aren’t a\nton of muons around - so if you want a reliable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203210,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supply of muons, you pretty much have to make\nthem with a high energy particle accelerator"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207240,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": ", which takes a lot of energy per muon - at\nbest about 5 giga electron volts , or about 50"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212900,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "times the E=mc^2 mass-energy of a muon itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217020,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, luckily you don’t need a muon for every\nsingle pair of hydrogen nuclei you want to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221600,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fuse, because after a pair of nuclei fuses\ninto helium the muon can go off and help more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226840,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nuclei fuse …and then help more… and more…\nand more…."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231740,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "EXCEPT, every so often , the muon doesn’t\n- it’ll get stuck as part of the newly fused"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237050,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "helium atom , and can’t facilitate any additional\nfusing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240740,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This means that each muon only helps an average\nof 150- fusions of nuclei before it gets stuck"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246130,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": ". And since each fusion of nuclei releases\nabout 18 mega electron volts of energy , this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251800,
    "dDurationMs": 6689,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means that, after 150 fusions, each muon facilitates\nan average of 2700 mega electron volts, or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258489,
    "dDurationMs": 3291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2.7 giga electron volts, of energy generation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261780,
    "dDurationMs": 4719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which means that, unfortunately, the numbers\ndon’t add up - Remember it currently takes around"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266499,
    "dDurationMs": 6461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "5 GeV of energy to produce a muon, but each\nmuon only generates about two and a half GeV"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272960,
    "dDurationMs": 2660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of energy before getting stuck to a nucleus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275620,
    "dDurationMs": 5019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That is, muon-facilitated fusion is a net\nconsumer of energy (rather than being a source"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280639,
    "dDurationMs": 1011,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of energy)."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281650,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the best case possible with current\ntechnology, and the numbers are still off"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285900,
    "dDurationMs": 4889,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by a factor of 2 before even reaching any\nsort of break-even where muon-facilitated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290789,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fusion could generate as much energy as it\nconsumes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293789,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we’d need to be much better than just\nbreaking even, energy-wise, to make a viable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297949,
    "dDurationMs": 1611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "commercial power plant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299560,
    "dDurationMs": 5659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pretty much the only hope for muon-facilitated-fusion\nis to figure out how to make muons for less"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305219,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy, or figure out how to have less of\nthem stick to the helium nuclei, or how to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309039,
    "dDurationMs": 5511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unstick them once they’re stuck - which\nare all hard problems limited by the unchangeable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314550,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physical properties of muons and nuclei, and\nso we’ve made quite slow progress in over"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319740,
    "dDurationMs": 1570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "70 years of research."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321310,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The summary is that muon-induced fusion exists,\nit’s fascinating science, but it’s not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326620,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "going to be powering the world any time soon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331720,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To dive deeper into the energy sources that\nDO power the world, I highly recommend checking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336680,
    "dDurationMs": 4819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out the “Fuel the World” course on Brilliant.org,\nthis video’s sponsor."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341499,
    "dDurationMs": 3591,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fuel the world is part of their series on\nthe “Physics of the everyday”, and guides"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345090,
    "dDurationMs": 5319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you through the basics of solar power, fossil\nfuels, nuclear reactions, dyson spheres, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350409,
    "dDurationMs": 1991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how much energy mammals need to survive."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352400,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s even a section about the fusion\nreactions that happen in the sun!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356240,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the “physics of the everyday” course\nas a whole gives a great overview of the physics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360080,
    "dDurationMs": 3839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of household items, sports, weather and climate,\nand more."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363919,
    "dDurationMs": 6041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the first 200 people to go to Brilliant.org/minutephysics\ncan get 20% off of a premium Brilliant subscription"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369960,
    "dDurationMs": 2849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with access to all of brilliant’s courses\nand puzzles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372809,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Again, that's Brilliant.org/minutephysics\nwhich gets you 20% off premium access so you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377669,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can seriously hone your math and science skills,\nand it lets Brilliant know you came from here."
    } ]
  } ]
}
